WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.593
Password storage is not the same as hashing a file.

2
00:00:03.593 --> 00:00:07.545
Passwords are often human-chosen and lower entropy than random keys.

3
00:00:07.545 --> 00:00:15.449
A fast general cryptographic hash can be excellent for fingerprints while still being the wrong construction for stored passwords because speed helps

4
00:00:15.449 --> 00:00:16.527
large-scale guessing.

5
00:00:16.527 --> 00:00:22.275
Password storage needs mechanisms designed for that purpose, not an ordinary digest wrapped in local conventions.

6
00:00:22.275 --> 00:00:26.945
Approved password hashing or key-derivation mechanisms use salts and configurable cost parameters.

7
00:00:26.945 --> 00:00:33.053
A salt helps ensure that identical passwords do not produce identical stored values across accounts or systems.

8
00:00:33.053 --> 00:00:40.598
Work factors and memory cost are intended to make verification acceptable for the legitimate service while making bulk guessing more expensive.

9
00:00:40.598 --> 00:00:47.065
Those parameters should be stored with the verifier so they can be upgraded over time as policy changes.

10
00:00:47.065 --> 00:00:50.298
C++ applications should keep password handling narrow and careful.

11
00:00:50.298 --> 00:00:54.609
Do not log passwords, password-derived values, reset secrets, or verification material.

12
00:00:54.609 --> 00:00:57.484
Keep storage formats versioned enough to support migration.

13
00:00:57.484 --> 00:01:05.388
Separate password verifier logic from general hashing utilities so a developer cannot accidentally choose a fast digest because the helper was easy

14
00:01:05.388 --> 00:01:06.106
to call.

15
00:01:06.106 --> 00:01:12.214
Review should ask whether salts are generated correctly, parameters are current, and upgrade behavior has been tested.

16
00:01:12.214 --> 00:01:19.040
The verification path should also handle legacy records deliberately so old formats do not remain accepted forever by accident.
